00:02:00.018 --> 00:02:02.525
- [Voiceover] Galen asks,
"How would you market

00:02:02.525 --> 00:02:05.033
"to Amish people if that
was your primary market?

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:07.657
"#BuggyHustle."

00:02:07.657 --> 00:02:09.235
- Galen, this is a tremendous question,

00:02:09.235 --> 00:02:10.651
and I think it opens up
an opportunity for me

00:02:10.651 --> 00:02:11.976
to give a broad answer.

00:02:11.976 --> 00:02:15.180
The truth is, I'd probably
spend six months to a year

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.177
before I would say a word or market,

00:02:17.177 --> 00:02:19.475
for me personally, because I completely

00:02:19.475 --> 00:02:22.285
am undereducated on that market.

00:02:22.285 --> 00:02:24.932
I don't know what makes that market tick,

00:02:24.932 --> 00:02:26.487
what do they, you know, obviously,

00:02:26.487 --> 00:02:28.182
what, if anything, are they buying?

00:02:28.182 --> 00:02:31.247
You know, obviously I'm
not deeply knowledgeable.

00:02:31.247 --> 00:02:33.987
Obviously I have headlines on what I think

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:36.332
is going on there, preconceived notions,

00:02:36.332 --> 00:02:39.537
but I've learned in my 40 years of life

00:02:39.537 --> 00:02:42.253
those tend to lead you to disaster

00:02:42.253 --> 00:02:44.830
when you're making
assumptions on the market

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.036
when you don't intimately know it.

00:02:47.036 --> 00:02:48.963
You know, for me, the two core businesses

00:02:48.963 --> 00:02:51.169
I've run in my career, the wine business

00:02:51.169 --> 00:02:53.584
and an agency that markets
within social media,

00:02:53.584 --> 00:02:55.883
if you pay attention
closely, they were markets

00:02:55.883 --> 00:02:58.460
that I spent five to seven, eight years

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.130
being deeply in before I took the reins.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.615
One, obviously, in a family
business growing up in it,

00:03:03.615 --> 00:03:05.983
and two, really participating in it,

00:03:05.983 --> 00:03:07.864
being a thought leader
within it, jamming with it,

00:03:07.864 --> 00:03:11.416
using it, and I mean, I
was using social media

00:03:11.416 --> 00:03:15.247
hardcore for, you know, five, six years

00:03:15.247 --> 00:03:19.171
before I started VaynerMedia
to then give advice around it,

00:03:19.171 --> 00:03:20.820
and so that's what I would
do, and that's what I think

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.933
a lot of you are making huge mistakes on.

00:03:22.933 --> 00:03:24.790
Interacting with so many
of you on Instagram,

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:27.623
Facebook, Twitter, I notice this quick,

00:03:27.623 --> 00:03:29.759
you know, I wanna sell sneakers, right?

00:03:29.759 --> 00:03:31.059
I wanna be in the sneaker game,

00:03:31.059 --> 00:03:32.638
and yeah, you kinda like sneakers,

00:03:32.638 --> 00:03:35.295
but do you understand
the sneaker business?

00:03:36.313 --> 00:03:38.478
And I went with the huge pause there,

00:03:38.478 --> 00:03:40.730
Staphon, for a specific reason:

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.796
When you decide to jump into business,

00:03:42.796 --> 00:03:44.817
yeah, you may like wine, but do you

00:03:44.817 --> 00:03:46.535
understand the wine business?

00:03:46.535 --> 00:03:48.973
Yeah, you may have a Twitter account,

00:03:48.973 --> 00:03:51.364
but do you understand
social media business?

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:54.383
Yeah, you may have some kicks
and have six or seven pairs

00:03:54.383 --> 00:03:58.260
or even 50, but do you
understand the sneaker business?

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.536
And so if I'm marketing, I'm in business,

00:04:00.536 --> 00:04:02.277
and if I don't understand the market,

00:04:02.277 --> 00:04:05.203
if I don't deeply understand
the competitive landscape,

00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:06.921
the supply and demand chain,

00:04:06.921 --> 00:04:08.825
what I'm gonna do, the
marketing opportunities,

00:04:08.825 --> 00:04:11.356
the white space that I
love to play in so much,

00:04:11.356 --> 00:04:13.932
then I'm gonna lose, and
that's why so many people lose.

00:04:13.932 --> 00:04:17.791
They confuse enthusiasm or hobby

00:04:17.791 --> 00:04:20.439
for business skills, and
so before you do anything,

00:04:20.439 --> 00:04:22.297
before you jump into anything,

00:04:22.297 --> 00:04:24.154
I highly recommend, you know
so many of you are starting

00:04:24.154 --> 00:04:27.637
T-shirt companies without
doing a hardcore audit

00:04:27.637 --> 00:04:30.725
of the 50 hundred T-shirt
companies in the market,

00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:32.838
and what they're doing and
how much they're spending.

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:35.402
Do your damn homework.

00:04:36.298 --> 00:04:38.426
- [Voiceover] Daniel asks,
I've started a startup

00:04:38.426 --> 00:04:41.004
that many others have tried
to do before me &amp;amp; failed.

00:04:41.004 --> 00:04:43.650
Am I stupid to think
that I'll be the anomaly?

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.462
- Daniel, that's a tough question,

00:04:45.462 --> 00:04:48.271
because the truth is,
there are some mountains

00:04:48.271 --> 00:04:49.641
that are very difficult to climb

00:04:49.641 --> 00:04:52.334
and thesis's that people
love to think are real,

00:04:52.334 --> 00:04:55.074
and they're never able to
really fully get there.

00:04:55.074 --> 00:04:57.675
I think the anonymous social network space

00:04:57.675 --> 00:04:59.439
is gonna end up being one of them,

00:04:59.439 --> 00:05:01.576
I think that brings
out the worst in people

00:05:01.576 --> 00:05:04.617
and ultimately kills
itself from within itself.

00:05:04.617 --> 00:05:07.938
That's one preview to a space
that I'm not bullish on,

00:05:07.938 --> 00:05:09.052
that I think a lot of people are gonna try

00:05:09.052 --> 00:05:10.979
to climb that mountain, that said,

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:13.464
it just takes one tweak, one turn.

00:05:13.464 --> 00:05:16.738
You know, there was a lot of industries

00:05:16.738 --> 00:05:19.756
where people couldn't
figure out how to win,

00:05:19.756 --> 00:05:22.751
and then eventually somebody
came along and won that game,

00:05:22.751 --> 00:05:26.559
and so I think that very
similar to the first answer

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:30.251
in this episode, it
comes down to your chops,

00:05:30.251 --> 00:05:33.084
your skills, your patience,
your infrastructure,

00:05:33.084 --> 00:05:36.033
and really really really does
come down to your skill set.

00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:39.051
I really do believe that, you know,

00:05:39.051 --> 00:05:42.139
anybody can do anything
if they're good enough,

00:05:42.139 --> 00:05:44.531
if they're the first of that thing.

00:05:44.531 --> 00:05:46.922
I just think that people
have to be very realistic

00:05:46.922 --> 00:05:50.005
to also understand how special
and rare that actually is.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.121
- [Voiceover] Nathan
asks, "I'm in the middle

00:05:53.121 --> 00:05:54.956
"of transitioning to a new job.

00:05:54.956 --> 00:05:56.442
"I was wondering what your advice would be

00:05:56.442 --> 00:05:58.601
"for my last two weeks?"

00:05:58.601 --> 00:06:00.598
- Nathan, I'm gonna give
you some really good advice

00:06:00.598 --> 00:06:02.316
that I think a lot of
people here need to hear

00:06:02.316 --> 00:06:04.267
as you transition from job to job.

00:06:04.267 --> 00:06:07.285
I actually think the last
two weeks of your job

00:06:07.285 --> 00:06:10.002
are one of the most interesting times

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:11.882
you can have in your career.

00:06:11.882 --> 00:06:13.647
It's something that I've
spent a lot of time on,

00:06:13.647 --> 00:06:15.758
paying attention to the behavior
of people that have spent

00:06:15.758 --> 00:06:19.126
last two weeks at Wine
Library or VaynerMedia.

00:06:19.126 --> 00:06:22.682
I think that your actions
in those two weeks

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:24.934
will really be much more impactful

00:06:24.934 --> 00:06:27.418
on your future career
than you may realize.

00:06:27.418 --> 00:06:30.831
I think the grace and being
the bigger man or woman,

00:06:30.831 --> 00:06:34.407
and humility, and all the
things that come along with it,

00:06:34.407 --> 00:06:37.379
are all those great traits
that I like to talk about

00:06:37.379 --> 00:06:42.209
pretty often, are so imperative
in those last two weeks,

00:06:42.209 --> 00:06:43.973
because everybody's watching.

00:06:43.973 --> 00:06:45.692
Your coworkers are watching,

00:06:45.692 --> 00:06:47.410
and definitely your employer is watching.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.175
It's stunning to me how many people

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:52.303
have come back around at a
year later at VaynerMedia,

00:06:52.303 --> 00:06:55.257
asking me for help, asking for a job back,

00:06:55.257 --> 00:06:58.066
when they were actually
terrible, or not terrible,

00:06:58.066 --> 00:06:59.761
that's not fair 'cause
I'm very competitive.

00:06:59.761 --> 00:07:02.873
You know, they were, oh I'm
going to this great gig,

00:07:02.873 --> 00:07:04.498
and like trying to impact other people

00:07:04.498 --> 00:07:05.659
to make them realize how they're going

00:07:05.659 --> 00:07:09.490
to this amazing place, and it's funny

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:11.766
how the grass is not as green

00:07:11.766 --> 00:07:14.157
once you land on your new location,

00:07:14.157 --> 00:07:16.247
and then when you're lucky enough to work

00:07:16.247 --> 00:07:19.149
for somebody who's a good
guy and that has some power

00:07:19.149 --> 00:07:22.446
and then you go ask for
something, all you had to do

00:07:22.446 --> 00:07:24.722
was be the bigger woman
for those two weeks

00:07:24.722 --> 00:07:26.695
and I would go out of my way to help you.

00:07:26.695 --> 00:07:29.482
I think way too many people
burn bridges on the way out,

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:34.195
bridges that they need,
because the shortsighted nature

00:07:34.195 --> 00:07:36.872
of our society, much of the
themes of the 200 episodes,

00:07:36.872 --> 00:07:39.217
or 150 episodes, or 120 episodes.

00:07:39.217 --> 00:07:40.657
Lou, how are you my man?

00:07:40.657 --> 00:07:42.050
Come give this man a little bit of daps

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:43.930
on the #AskGaryVee show,
I know he watches it.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.184
How are you? Thanks for having me on.

00:07:45.184 --> 00:07:47.344
- I've been jabbin' away on social media

00:07:47.344 --> 00:07:50.200
saying local TV will never be the same.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:51.235
- I love it, I'm excited.

00:07:51.235 --> 00:07:53.307
Let me finish this show,
I'll be with you in a second.

00:07:54.537 --> 00:07:56.790
I think burning those
bridges is a devastating play

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:58.439
that way too many people feel,

00:07:58.439 --> 00:08:00.760
out of excitement sometimes, or out of,

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:03.101
you know, cynicism and
all the negative things

00:08:03.101 --> 00:08:04.889
that then kinda makes
me feel that it's fair

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:07.490
that they're unable to
mend those relationships

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:09.417
and get value out of
those past relationships.

00:08:09.417 --> 00:08:12.203
To me, there's no value in anything

00:08:12.203 --> 00:08:15.338
other than being over the top awesome,

00:08:15.338 --> 00:08:18.542
giving love to everybody,
making amends with people

00:08:18.542 --> 00:08:20.817
maybe that you didn't work
well with, the last two weeks.

00:08:20.817 --> 00:08:22.348
I think you treat it like the last month

00:08:22.348 --> 00:08:24.489
of high school, senior year, right?

00:08:24.489 --> 00:08:26.172
You just make it all good.

00:08:27.392 --> 00:08:28.963
- [Voiceover] Nils asks,
"If you were to make

00:08:28.963 --> 00:08:30.798
"a video game, what type would it be

00:08:30.798 --> 00:08:32.556
"and on what platform 
would you sell it?"

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:35.278
- Nils, this is a pretty
interesting question.

00:08:35.278 --> 00:08:36.996
There's two spaces that
I think video games

00:08:36.996 --> 00:08:39.294
are really interesting, and
one is obviously mobile.

00:08:39.294 --> 00:08:42.082
I still think mobile,
kind of word, simple,

00:08:42.082 --> 00:08:44.311
like, the game that
works for the 13 year old

00:08:44.311 --> 00:08:46.702
and for the 84 year old grandmother

00:08:46.702 --> 00:08:48.351
are always super interesting to me,

00:08:48.351 --> 00:08:51.927
very basic, you know, just simple word,

00:08:51.927 --> 00:08:55.247
brain quizzes, you know, Hangman 3.0,

00:08:55.247 --> 00:08:56.895
that kinda stuff is super interesting.

00:08:56.895 --> 00:08:58.010
But the place that I'd probably go

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:00.657
since I love so much the game of following

00:09:00.657 --> 00:09:03.048
where I think the puck is going to be,

00:09:03.048 --> 00:09:07.460
is I'd probably make a
virtual reality game,

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:10.773
one that had, you know,
kind of like the Zelda of VR,

00:09:10.773 --> 00:09:12.746
which was a completely open world

00:09:12.746 --> 00:09:14.139
where you could do a whole lot of stuff.

00:09:14.139 --> 00:09:15.477
I don't know if I would go shooter,

00:09:15.477 --> 00:09:18.092
though I think that's gonna
be the breakout win early on,

00:09:19.108 --> 00:09:22.184
I'm a little bit more into like,
Gauntlet really affected me

00:09:22.184 --> 00:09:23.531
in the early days, it
was like one of the first

00:09:23.531 --> 00:09:25.528
four player arcade games
that me and my boys

00:09:25.528 --> 00:09:28.221
back in '84 would roll into Chuck E Cheese

00:09:28.221 --> 00:09:31.124
with quarters for days
and just get down with it.

00:09:31.124 --> 00:09:33.074
And I was always pumped to
be the princess, by the way,

00:09:33.074 --> 00:09:34.235
everybody would fight for the wizard,

00:09:34.235 --> 00:09:36.371
I'm like I'll be princess, she was nasty.

00:09:36.371 --> 00:09:40.156
Anyway, so I would say
some sorta open-ended

00:09:40.156 --> 00:09:42.595
Zelda-Gauntlet adventure type game,

00:09:42.595 --> 00:09:45.636
for virtual reality, that
I don't think would pop

00:09:45.636 --> 00:09:47.609
for another 24 to 36 months,

00:09:47.609 --> 00:09:49.652
but if you follow the
narrative of my 20 year career

00:09:49.652 --> 00:09:51.928
and hopefully what I do
for the next 40 years,

00:09:51.928 --> 00:09:53.925
I'm always going to bet on where I think

00:09:53.925 --> 00:09:56.804
the market's gonna be
24, 36 months from now

00:09:56.804 --> 00:09:59.079
use my disproportional ability to sell,

00:09:59.079 --> 00:10:02.051
to keep me afloat for
those 24 to 36 months,

00:10:02.051 --> 00:10:03.955
and then be there where the
market's gonna be there.

00:10:03.955 --> 00:10:06.788
When I started selling social
media to brands in 2009,

00:10:06.788 --> 00:10:07.972
they didn't wanna buy it.

00:10:07.972 --> 00:10:11.594
Only now, only as we
head into 2016 budgets,

00:10:11.594 --> 00:10:13.498
am I starting to see some real upside.

00:10:13.498 --> 00:10:16.285
And as you can imagine, that
bodes well for my business.

00:10:16.285 --> 00:10:19.074
I would do the same thing
in the video game industry.

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:21.621
- [Staphon] The stream
just shut down.

00:10:21.621 --> 00:10:24.951
- Why? Just crashed on you? Cool.

00:10:24.951 --> 00:10:26.822
- [India] So, Britt.

00:10:29.103 --> 00:10:31.388
- Hey Gary, this is Britt Michaelian,

00:10:31.388 --> 00:10:35.567
and my question for you
for the #AskGaryVee Show is,

00:10:35.567 --> 00:10:38.400
what women in business do you think

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.652
are just rockin' it on social media?

00:10:40.652 --> 00:10:42.951
I love to follow people
who are doin' great stuff

00:10:42.951 --> 00:10:44.785
and I'd love to know who you think

00:10:44.785 --> 00:10:47.376
is doing an exceptional
job on social media.

00:10:47.376 --> 00:10:49.912
Women in business, thank
you, have a great day.

00:10:49.912 --> 00:10:51.631
- Women in business, social media.

00:10:51.631 --> 00:10:53.720
This is tough for me
for a couple of reasons.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.575
One, I really don't follow anybody.

00:10:57.575 --> 00:10:59.363
You know, all I'm really doing

00:10:59.363 --> 00:11:02.195
is putting out content and
engaging with my own audience

00:11:02.195 --> 00:11:04.192
and so I'm not trying
to duck the question,

00:11:04.192 --> 00:11:07.002
obviously there's plenty
of people that pop to mind

00:11:07.002 --> 00:11:09.834
that I think are incredible
entrepreneurs and business women

00:11:09.834 --> 00:11:14.107
you know, Katia, CEO of
Birchbox, an investment of mine.

00:11:14.107 --> 00:11:15.662
Obviously since I'm an investor in it,

00:11:15.662 --> 00:11:17.195
I'm a little bit closer to it.

00:11:17.195 --> 00:11:19.888
I think she's an incredible
